L000894	
L000895	\subsection{Micromagnetic model}
L000896	
L000897	
L000898	
L000899	\subsubsection{Magnon dispersion at equilibrium}
L000900	We consider a uniformly magnetized rectangular film with in–plane dimensions $l=3~\mathrm{mm}$ and $w=2~\mathrm{mm}$ and thickness $d=23~\mu\mathrm{m}$. The saturation magnetization is estimated as $m_s \approx 0.185\ \text{T}$. In the presence of a static bias field $\mathbf H_0=H_0\,\hat{\mathbf z}$ and a longitudinal parametric drive of amplitude $H_1$ and angular frequency $\Omega$ (collinear with $\mathbf H_0$), the magnetization dynamics are governed by the Landau–Lifshitz–Gilbert (LLG) equation
L000901	\begin{equation}
L000902	\dot{\mathbf m}
L000903	= -\gamma\,\mathbf m \times \!\Big[\mathbf H_0 + \mathbf h_d[\mathbf m] + H_1\cos(\Omega t)\,\hat{\mathbf z}\Big]
L000904	+ \alpha\,\mathbf m \times \dot{\mathbf m},
L000905	\label{eq_s1}
L000906	\end{equation}
L000907	where $\gamma = 28\ \text{GHz/T}$ is the gyromagnetic ratio and $\alpha\ll1$ is the (dimensionless) Gilbert damping parameter. The magnetostatic (demagnetizing) field generated by dipolar interactions reads as
L000908	\begin{equation}
L000909	\mathbf h_d(\mathbf r,t)=\int d^3 r'\,\boldsymbol\Gamma(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')\cdot\mathbf m(\mathbf r',t), \label{eq_s1.2}
L000910	\end{equation}
L000911	where $\boldsymbol\Gamma$ is the magnetostatic Green tensor \cite{guslienko2011magnetostatic}. The relevant modes lie in the magnetostatic, long-wavelength regime, where dipolar contributions dominate and exchange is parametrically small in micron-scale YIG films.
L000912	In this regime, the lowest-order thickness mode is nearly uniform along $y$,
L000913	so the full Green tensor can be replaced by its thickness average.
L000914	Since $d \ll \{l, w\}$, variations across the thickness are negligible compared
L000915	to the in-plane dimensions, leading to
L000916	\begin{equation}
L000917	\Gamma_{\alpha\beta}(\boldsymbol{\rho},\boldsymbol{\rho}')
L000918	= \frac{1}{d} \int_0^d \! dy \int_0^d \! dy'\,
L000919	\Gamma_{\alpha\beta}(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}'),\label{kernal}
L000920	\end{equation}
L000921	where $\mathbf r=(\boldsymbol\rho,y)$, with  $\boldsymbol\rho=(z,x)$. Linearizing the LLG equation (\ref{eq_s1}) around the equilibrium configuration, substituting the averaged kernel from Eq.~(\ref{kernal}), and solving the resulting eigenvalue equation in the Fourier domain yield the standard magnetostatic dispersion relation for the fundamental film mode~\cite{jorzick1999brillouin}:
L000922	\begin{equation}
L000923	\omega_{\mathbf k}=\gamma\sqrt{\Big(H_0+m_s\,\frac{k_x^2}{k^2}f(kd)\Big)
L000924	\Big(H_0+m_s\,[1-f(kd)]\Big)} ,
L000925	\quad f(kd)=1-\frac{1-e^{-kd}}{kd},
L000926	\end{equation}
L000927	with $k=\sqrt{k_x^2+k_z^2}$. In our device the loop antenna is aligned parallel to the long edge and excites/detects waves that propagate in–plane perpendicular to $\mathbf H_0$, i.e., Damon–Eshbach–type MSSWs along $\hat{\mathbf x}$ \cite{eshbach1960surface}. That is, we set $k_z=0$ and $k \equiv k_x$. The finite width imposes mixed (Robin) boundary conditions on the transverse
L000928	magnetization $m=m_x+im_y$
L000929	at $x=\pm l/2$~\cite{jackson2021classical}:
L000930	\begin{equation}
L000931	\partial_x m|_{x=\pm l/2}=\mp {\zeta_\pm}\,m|_{x=\pm l/2},
L000932	\end{equation}
L000933	with $\zeta_\pm$ set by the electromagnetic environment at each edge. Within the slab interior \((|y|<d/2)\), modes propagating perpendicular to \(\mathbf{H}_0\) are described by the spatial profile
L000934	\begin{equation}
L000935	\Phi(y,x)\propto
L000936	\begin{cases}
L000937	e^{k(d/2-y)} \,e^{i k x}, & k_x=k,\\[3pt]
L000938	e^{k(d/2+y)}\,e^{-i k x}, & k_x=-k.
L000939	\end{cases}  \label{eq_s8}
L000940	\end{equation}
L000941	so that opposite propagation directions localize on opposite film surfaces~\cite{eshbach1960surface}. In the magnetostatic interior, Maxwell’s equations reduce to the 2D Laplace equation \(\partial_x^2\Phi+\partial_y^2\Phi=0\), which implies \(k_y^2=-k_x^2\) and thus exponential decay across \(y\)~\cite{hurben1995theory}. However, in the long-wavelength limit \(kd\ll1\), the thickness dependence is weak: the profile is nearly uniform across the thickness, and standing waves can develop along \(x\) \cite{hurben1995theory}.
L000942	 The generalized Robin boundary conditions, together with the mode-profile ansatz
L000943	$\Phi(u) = A\cos(ku) + B\sin(ku)$ (with $u = x + l/2$), yield the following expressions
L000944	for the corresponding mode profiles:
L000945	\begin{equation}
L000946	\Phi_n(u)=N_n\big(\cos(k_nu)+\frac{\zeta}{k_n}\sin(k_nu)\big), \label{eq_s10}
L000947	\end{equation}
L000948	where $k_n$  is obtained from the transcendental equation
L000949	\begin{equation}
L000950	    (\zeta_{+}\zeta_{-}-k^{2})\,\sin(kl)+k(\zeta_{+}+\zeta_{-})\,\cos(kl)=0,
L000951	\end{equation}
L000952	and $N_n$ is a normalization constant. For $\zeta_+ = -800~\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and $\zeta_- = 1500~\mathrm{m^{-1}}$,
L000953	the wave numbers are approximately given by $k_n \approx n\pi / l$. The frequency of the lowest mode $\omega_n\equiv\omega_{k_n}$ $\sim 4.067\,\mathrm{GHz}$ at \(H_0 \approx 76.5~\mathrm{mT}\) corresponds to \(n=3\) with \(k_3 \approx 3.14\times10^{3}~\mathrm{m^{-1}}\), yielding \(kd \approx 0.072 \ll 1\), which corroborates the validity of the magnetostatic, thickness-averaged approximation used in this derivation.
L000954	
L000955	
L000956	\subsubsection{Driven dynamics}
L000957	
L000958	In the presence of the external parametric drive, the linearized LL equation becomes
L000959	\begin{equation}
L000960	\begin{bmatrix}
L000961	\dot{m^x} \\
L000962	\dot{m^y}
L000963	\end{bmatrix}
L000964	= -\gamma
L000965	\begin{bmatrix}
L000966	0 & H_0 + H_1 \cos(\Omega t) - m_s \Gamma_{yy}(\bold{k}) \\
L000967	- H_0 - H_1 \cos(\Omega t)+ \Gamma_{xx}(\bold{k}) m_s  & 0
L000968	\end{bmatrix}
L000969	\begin{bmatrix}
L000970	m^x \\
L000971	m^y
L000972	\end{bmatrix}.\label{eq_s11}
L000973	\end{equation}
L000974	
L000975	Since the strength of the ac drive \(H_1\) is significantly weaker than the static bias field, we treat \(H_1\cos(\Omega t)\) as a weak perturbation. To first order, the mode frequency is modulated as
L000976	\begin{equation}
L000977	\omega_n(t)=\omega_n+\left.\frac{\partial\omega_n}{\partial H_0}\right|_{H_0} H_1\cos(\Omega t)
L000978	=\omega_n+\Delta\cos(\Omega t),
L000979	\label{eq_s12}
L000980	\end{equation}
L000981	with \(\Delta\equiv \left.\frac{\partial\omega_n}{\partial H_0}\right|_{H_0} H_1\) being the Floquet-induced magnon mode frequency deviation from equilibrium. The parametric drive imposes a periodic, first-order modulation of the normal-mode frequencies, which is taken to be mode independent (i.e., independent of $n$),  and weakly distorts their spatial profiles.
L000982	 The profile distortion feeds back through the demagnetizing Green tensor: the modulated magnetization generates oscillatory dipolar fields [Eq.~(\ref{eq_s1.2})], which, in turn, drive the dynamics of the complex transverse amplitude via
L000983	\begin{equation}
L000984	\dot m(\mathbf r,t)=-i\,\gamma m_s\big[h_d^x(\mathbf r,t)+i\,h_d^y(\mathbf r,t)\big].
L000985	\label{eq_s14}
L000986	\end{equation}
L000987	
L000988	
L000989	
L000990	
L000991	
L000992	We expand the transverse magnetization in the eigenmode basis of the undriven system
L000993	\begin{equation}
L000994	{m}(t) = \sum_n a_n(t) \Phi_n(u),\label{eq_s15}
L000995	\end{equation}
L000996	\(a_n(t)\) are slowly varying modal envelopes defined in a frame rotating at \(\omega_n\).  Plugging Eq. (\ref{eq_s15}) in Eq. (\ref{eq_s14}) and invoking the rotating wave approximation leads to
L000997	\begin{align}
L000998	\dot{a}_m(t) &= - \frac{i \gamma m_s}{2} \sum_{n \neq m} \left( G^x_{nm} + i G^y_{nm} \right)a_n(t). \label{eq_s16}
L000999	\end{align}
L001000	The coupling coefficients in Eq. (\ref{eq_s16}) are matrix elements of the  thickness- and width–averaged magnetostatic Green tensor~\cite{guslienko2011magnetostatic} in the modal basis,
L001001	\begin{equation}
L001002	G_{n m}^y = \iint \Phi_m(x)\,\Gamma_{yy}(x-x')\,\Phi_{n}(x')\,dx\,dx',
L001003	\qquad
L001004	G_{n m}^x = \iint \Phi_m(x)\,\Gamma_{xx}(x-x')\,\Phi_{n}(x')\,dx\,dx',
L001005	\label{eq_s17}
L001006	\end{equation}
L001007	with kernels
L001008	\begin{equation}
L001009	\begin{aligned}
L001010	\Gamma_{yy}(x-x') &= \frac{4}{dl}\Bigg[
L001011	l\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\frac{l}{\sqrt{d^2+\xi^2}}\right)
L001012	- l\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\frac{l}{\xi}\right)
L001013	+ \sqrt{d^2+\xi^2}-\xi \\[3pt]
L001014	&\quad
L001015	+ \sqrt{l^2+\xi^2}-\sqrt{d^2+l^2+\xi^2}
L001016	\Bigg], \\[6pt]
L001017	\Gamma_{zz}(x-x') &= \frac{4}{dl}\Bigg[
L001018	d\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\frac{d}{\sqrt{l^2+\xi^2}}\right)
L001019	- d\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\frac{d}{\xi}\right)
L001020	+ \sqrt{l^2+\xi^2}-\xi \\[3pt]
L001021	&\quad
L001022	+ \sqrt{d^2+\xi^2}-\sqrt{d^2+l^2+\xi^2}
L001023	\Bigg], \\[6pt]
L001024	\Gamma_{xx}(x-x') &= - \delta(x-x') - \Gamma_{zz}(x-x') - \Gamma_{yy}(x-x'),
L001025	\end{aligned}
L001026	\label{eq_s18}
L001027	\end{equation}
L001028	where $\xi=|x-x'|$. The diagonal coupling terms \(n=m\) renormalize the mode frequencies by a parametrically small self-energy shift, which is neglected henceforth.
L001029	
L001030	In the absence of periodic driving, the laboratory-frame modal amplitudes carry the carrier phase \(e^{-i\omega_n t}\).
L001031	When the frequency is parametrically modulated according to Eq.~(\ref{eq_s12}), the source term on the right-hand side of Eq.~(\ref{eq_s16}) is modified as $a_n(t) \to a_n(t)\, e^{-i  \tfrac{\Delta}{\Omega} \sin \Omega t }$, where the exponential’s frequency argument follows from integrating Eq.~(\ref{eq_s12}) over the interval $0$ to $t$. Expanding $a_m$ in terms of the Fourier harmonics
L001032	\begin{align}
L001033	a_m(t) &= \sum_k a_m^k e^{ik\Omega t},\label{eq_s19}
L001034	\end{align}
L001035	and plugging Eq.~(\ref{eq_s19}) into Eq. (\ref{eq_s16}), we obtain
L001036	\begin{align}
L001037	\sum_k \left( i k \Omega a_m^k + \dot{a}_m^k \right) e^{i k \Omega t}
L001038	&= -i \gamma m_s \frac{1}{2} \sum_{n \neq m} \sum_{\ell} \sum_{r}
L001039	\tilde{G}_{nm} a_n^\ell \mathcal{J}_r\!\left( \frac{\Delta}{\Omega} \right) e^{i (\ell - r) \Omega t}, \label{eq_s20}
L001040	\end{align}
L001041	where $\tilde{G}_{nm}=G^x_{nm} + i G^y_{nm}$ and we used the Jacobi-Anger expansion
L001042	\begin{equation}
L001043	e^{-i \left(  \frac{\Delta}{\Omega} \sin(\Omega t) \right)} = \sum_{\ell=-\infty}^{\infty} \mathcal{J}_{\ell}\left( \frac{\Delta}{\Omega}\right) e^{-i (\ell \Omega)t} .
L001044	\label{eq_s21}
L001045	\end{equation}
L001046	where $\mathcal{J}_{\ell}$ denote the Bessel functions of order $\ell$.
L001047	
L001048	
L001049	Projecting Eq. (\ref{eq_s20}) into $e^{i p \Omega t}$ and equating its coefficients on either side, yields the sideband-resolved dynamical equation
L001050	\begin{equation}
L001051	\left( i p \Omega \, a_m^p + \dot{a}_m^p \right)
L001052	= -i \gamma m_s \frac{1}{2} \sum_{n \neq m} \sum_{\ell \neq p}
L001053	\tilde{G}_{nm} \mathcal{J}_{\ell - p}\!\left( \frac{\Delta}{\Omega} \right)a_n^\ell \label{eq_s22}.
L001054	\end{equation}
L001055	Guided by the experimental observations, we restrict the model to nearest-neighbor (NN) couplings in $n$ and first order sidebands.
L001056	 Equation (\ref{eq_s22}) then becomes
L001057	\begin{equation}
L001058	    \left( i p \Omega \, a_m^p + \dot{a}_m^p \right)=-ig_{m,m+1}\!\left[a_{m+1}^{p-1}+a_{m+1}^{p+1}\right]
L001059	-i g_{m-1,m}\!\left[a_{m-1}^{p-1}+a_{m-1}^{p+1}\right],\label{eq_s22.1}
L001060	\end{equation}
L001061	where $g_{m,m+1}\approx \frac{\gamma m_s}{2}\tilde{G}_{m,m+1}\mathcal{J}_1(\Delta/\Omega)$.
L001062	
L001063	This restriction to nearest-neighbor coupling is justified by the strong suppression of higher-order processes in our experimental regime. The ratio between next-nearest-neighbor (NNN) and nearest-neighbor coupling strengths scales as $r = J_2(\Delta/\Omega)/J_1(\Delta/\Omega)$. For our experimental parameters with $\Delta/\Omega \approx 0.4$, this ratio is approximately $r \approx 0.1$, meaning NNN coupling is an order of magnitude weaker than NN coupling. The good agreement between the nearest-neighbor tight-binding model [Eq.~(2) in main text] and experimental observations in Fig.~3 provides further validation of this approximation.
L001064	
L001065	Summing Eq.~(\ref{eq_s22.1}) over \(p\)  and restoring the carrier phase yields
L001066	\begin{equation}
L001067	i\,\dot a_m(t)=\omega_m a_m(t)
L001068	+ 2\cos(\Omega t)\,\Big[\, g_{m,m+1}\,a_{m+1}(t)+g_{m-1,m}\,a_{m-1}(t)\,\Big].
L001069	\end{equation}
L001070	This corresponds to dynamics governed by the following time-dependent tight-binding Hamiltonian, in which we redefine $g_n \equiv g_{n,n+1}$:
L001071	\begin{equation}
L001072	\hat H=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\omega_n\,\hat a_n^\dagger\hat a_n
L001073	+\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}\Big[\,2\,g_{n,n+1}\cos(\Omega t)\,\hat a_n^\dagger\hat a_{n+1}+\text{h.c.}\,\Big].
L001074	\end{equation}
